WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.688 --> 00:00:19.798
As followed the strange ghost, the darkness of its cloak seemed to expand and swallow him up.

2
00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:25.221
He blinked and saw they were on a bustling city street.

3
00:00:26.922 --> 00:00:30.864
It was daytime, and several businessmen were gathered here and there, talking.

4
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.741
The spirit stopped beside three men.

5
00:00:34.761 --> 00:00:36.921
"Did you hear?"

6
00:00:37.581 --> 00:00:38.862
a stout man was saying.

7
00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:42.923
"The devil has welcomed back one of its own."

8
00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:45.204
"Yes, I heard.

9
00:00:45.224 --> 00:00:50.045
I don't know how it happened, though," a very tall man said.

10
00:00:50.065 --> 00:00:52.766
"I only know he's dead.

11
00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:54.646
When did he die?"

12
00:00:54.666 --> 00:00:58.027
"Last night, I believe."

13
00:00:58.047 --> 00:01:01.028
The third man checked his gold watch.

14
00:01:02.232 --> 00:01:04.173
"What was the matter with him anyway?

15
00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:06.813
I thought he'd never die."

16
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.714
"Who knows."

17
00:01:11.415 --> 00:01:12.535
The tall man yawned.

18
00:01:12.555 --> 00:01:16.316
"More importantly, what has he done with his money?"

19
00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:21.537
The man with the gold watch chuckled.

20
00:01:21.557 --> 00:01:25.618
"He didn't leave it to any of us, that's for sure!"

21
00:01:25.638 --> 00:01:31.340
"Well, it's going to be a very cheap and small funeral," the tall man said.

22
00:01:32.172 --> 00:01:35.015
"Should we go, just so at least a few people show up?"

23
00:01:37.177 --> 00:01:38.818
The stout man looked interested.

24
00:01:38.838 --> 00:01:43.342
"I'd go if a nice lunch is provided."

25
00:01:43.362 --> 00:01:49.508
"I know these men," Scrooge said to the ghost.

26
00:01:49.528 --> 00:01:52.451
"They're well known around the city.

27
00:01:52.471 --> 00:01:55.233
But why do you want me to hear them?

28
00:01:55.253 --> 00:01:57.756
Who are they discussing?

29
00:01:59.088 --> 00:02:02.910
It can't be Jacob Marley, because his death happened in the past.

30
00:02:03.871 --> 00:02:06.332
You're supposed to be showing me the future."

31
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:09.774
Suddenly the street disappeared.

32
00:02:10.895 --> 00:02:16.118
Scrooge was now walking through an unfamiliar part of the city with small, shabby houses.

33
00:02:17.339 --> 00:02:21.781
The streets were narrow and dirty, and the people looked tired and thin.

34
00:02:23.262 --> 00:02:27.624
Rats darted here and there, bold and bright eyed.

35
00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:32.548
Even the snow seemed different, with soot and dust settling upon it as soon as it touched the

36
00:02:32.568 --> 00:02:41.694
ground. The spirit led Scrooge to a grubby shop where iron, rags, bottles, and other items were bought

37
00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:42.235
and sold.

38
00:02:43.255 --> 00:02:46.938
A gray-haired shopkeeper sat among the goods, smoking a pipe.

39
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.622
A woman entered the shop carrying a large bundle.

40
00:02:54.263 --> 00:02:57.738
Moments later a second woman came in with a bundle of her own.

41
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.123
They were soon followed by a man in a faded black coat.

42
00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:08.569
When all three seemed startled to see the others, the shopkeeper laughed.

43
00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.974
"You look surprised to see one another," he said.

44
00:03:13.994 --> 00:03:20.500
"But I heard your employer died, and so I'm not surprised to see any of you.

45
00:03:21.848 --> 00:03:25.110
I'll let the housekeeper show me what she has brought first.

46
00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:28.232
The laundrywoman can go second.

47
00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:32.254
The undertaker's assistant will be third."

48
00:03:34.155 --> 00:03:36.836
The housekeeper dropped her bundle on the shop floor.

49
00:03:38.057 --> 00:03:40.418
Her expression was guilty and defiant.

50
00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:44.080
"Every person has a right to take care of themselves.

51
00:03:44.881 --> 00:03:47.782
Though I never met him, I know he always did."

52
00:03:47.802 --> 00:03:50.384
"That's true, indeed!"

53
00:03:50.816 --> 00:03:51.876
the laundrywoman said.

54
00:03:51.896 --> 00:03:55.097
"He was always thinking about himself!"

55
00:03:56.438 --> 00:03:58.318
The housekeeper kicked open her bundle.

56
00:03:59.498 --> 00:04:02.719
Inside were a couple of blankets and a set of bed curtains.

57
00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:06.340
"A dead man will not miss these things."

58
00:04:08.621 --> 00:04:13.182
"He won't miss these either," the laundrywoman said, opening her own bundle.

59
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:19.204
It contained men's clothing, a few silver teaspoons, and a worn pair of boots.

60
00:04:20.532 --> 00:04:24.554
"His things didn't help him in the end," the undertaker's assistant said.

61
00:04:24.574 --> 00:04:30.856
"They could not have comforted him while he lay gasping out his last breath, all by himself."

62
00:04:32.257 --> 00:04:33.557
The man opened the bundle.

63
00:04:34.378 --> 00:04:38.639
It held only a pencil case, some buttons, and a few other small things.

64
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.682
Scrooge watched in horror as the shopkeeper examined everything before him.

65
00:04:44.702 --> 00:04:48.883
"These servants stole from their employer!"

66
00:04:49.364 --> 00:04:50.104
Scrooge cried.

67
00:04:51.228 --> 00:04:54.470
"They have no respect for him, even when he's dead!"

68
00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.231
As usual, the spirit didn't answer.

69
00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.273
Scrooge sighed in frustration.

70
00:05:02.513 --> 00:05:06.355
Then he suddenly realized why the ghost was showing him these scenes.

71
00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:09.196
"Oh!" he said.

72
00:05:09.216 --> 00:05:16.560
"You're telling me, spirit, that the situation of this unfortunate man could be my situation too.

73
00:05:18.864 --> 00:05:23.528
You want me to know that I must change or I will share his fate.

74
00:05:24.329 --> 00:05:25.010
Is that correct?"

75
00:05:26.411 --> 00:05:30.334
The spirit lifted its finger, and the grubby shop disappeared.

76
00:05:31.555 --> 00:05:34.258
Now the two of them were standing in Scrooge's bedroom.

77
00:05:35.339 --> 00:05:39.702
A familiar form lay on the bed, not moving or even breathing.

78
00:05:39.722 --> 00:05:45.668
"Oh!" Scrooge gazed in horror at the lifeless body on the bed.

79
00:05:48.288 --> 00:05:52.530
"The dead man whom everyone is discussing—it's me!"

80
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.431
The spirit nodded slowly.

81
00:05:55.451 --> 00:05:59.572
"I promise, I have learned my lesson!"

82
00:06:00.052 --> 00:06:00.792
Scrooge said.

83
00:06:00.812 --> 00:06:06.754
"But surely there must be at least one person who will feel strong emotions about my death.

84
00:06:07.835 --> 00:06:10.756
If so, please show that person to me."
